WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.255
Lesson 4.7.

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:06.060
Stablecoin savings rates and yield-bearing stablecoins.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.544
By the end, you'll be able to tell apart

00:00:08.544 --> 00:00:12.118
four completely different ways a stablecoin can pay yield,

00:00:12.118 --> 00:00:14.420
and the specific risk behind each one.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.010
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.614
The four genuinely different places yield

00:00:19.614 --> 00:00:22.270
on stablecoins can actually come from.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.155
The specific risk that rides along with each one.

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:28.591
A full worked example, pricing a real, advertised rate

00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:32.015
against three real questions you should ask first.

00:00:32.015 --> 00:00:34.226
And a checklist for identifying, and

00:00:34.226 --> 00:00:36.915
diversifying across, these sources correctly.

00:00:38.215 --> 00:00:40.090
Four genuinely different sources.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.526
Here are all four places yield on

00:00:43.526 --> 00:00:47.345
a dollar-pegged stablecoin can genuinely originate from.

00:00:47.345 --> 00:00:51.644
Lending, where you're lending stablecoins directly to borrowers, exactly

00:00:51.644 --> 00:00:54.830
the lending markets covered back in Module three.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.544
Savings rates, a set rate some stablecoin protocols pay

00:00:58.544 --> 00:01:02.690
directly to holders, funded by the protocol's own revenue.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:06.594
Treasury-backed, where the issuer holds real, short-term government

00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:09.305
debt, and passes on part of that interest.

00:01:09.305 --> 00:01:12.418
And synthetic, or basis-backed, where the yield comes

00:01:12.418 --> 00:01:15.415
from a hedged derivatives position, earning funding.

00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:19.276
Here's each of those four, paired directly

00:01:19.276 --> 00:01:21.540
with its own specific risk, since the

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.580
risk is genuinely different every single time.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.799
Lending's risk is the protocol, the collateral backing

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:30.675
it, and the oracles pricing that collateral.

00:01:30.675 --> 00:01:34.466
Savings rates carry the stablecoin's own design and governance

00:01:34.466 --> 00:01:38.075
risk, since governance is what actually sets that rate.

00:01:38.075 --> 00:01:41.346
Treasury-backed carries the issuer, the custodian holding

00:01:41.346 --> 00:01:44.071
those treasuries, and the redemption terms.

00:01:44.071 --> 00:01:47.049
And synthetic, basis-backed yield carries funding

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:49.551
turning negative, and the exchange itself.

00:01:50.851 --> 00:01:53.502
Worth being precise about the actual lesson

00:01:53.502 --> 00:01:56.092
this comparison proves, since it's easy to

00:01:56.092 --> 00:01:58.501
see one number and assume one risk.

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:01.939
The exact same headline rate, somewhere between four and

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:04.533
ten percent, can be sitting on top of four

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:07.971
entirely different foundations, each one able to fail in

00:02:07.971 --> 00:02:11.711
a completely different way, for a completely different reason.

00:02:13.011 --> 00:02:15.757
Here's how to actually identify which of the

00:02:15.757 --> 00:02:19.236
four sources a specific stablecoin actually uses, rather

00:02:19.236 --> 00:02:21.916
than guessing from its marketing alone.

00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:25.404
Read its own documentation specifically for the words it

00:02:25.404 --> 00:02:28.708
uses to describe where the yield comes from, lending,

00:02:28.708 --> 00:02:32.006
a savings rate, treasuries, or a hedged position.

00:02:32.006 --> 00:02:34.792
Check whether it discloses what it's actually

00:02:34.792 --> 00:02:37.636
holding, or invested in, behind the token.

00:02:37.636 --> 00:02:40.841
And if none of that is genuinely disclosed anywhere,

00:02:40.841 --> 00:02:43.986
treat the yield source itself as unknown, and price

00:02:43.986 --> 00:02:46.526
that uncertainty as its own separate risk.

00:02:47.826 --> 00:02:49.927
Here's a rough, useful way to group

00:02:49.927 --> 00:02:51.970
these four, by how much their rate

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:54.021
tends to actually move around.

00:02:54.021 --> 00:02:57.596
Lending and treasury-backed yields tend to move relatively

00:02:57.596 --> 00:03:01.531
slowly, tracking broader interest-rate conditions over time.

00:03:01.531 --> 00:03:05.589
Protocol savings rates and synthetic, funding-based yields can

00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:09.003
move considerably faster, since a governance vote or

00:03:09.003 --> 00:03:11.902
a sharp shift in market sentiment can change

00:03:11.902 --> 00:03:13.641
either one in a single day.

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:16.031
Quick check.

00:03:16.031 --> 00:03:18.481
Name the four sources of stablecoin yield.

00:03:22.781 --> 00:03:26.199
The answer: lending, protocol savings rates, treasury

00:03:26.199 --> 00:03:29.491
backing, and synthetic basis, or funding, positions.

00:03:30.791 --> 00:03:32.626
A closer look at synthetic yield.

00:03:33.926 --> 00:03:37.911
Here's exactly how a synthetic, basis-backed yield actually gets

00:03:37.911 --> 00:03:41.521
generated, since it's the least intuitive of the four.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:44.331
The protocol holds a spot position, long

00:03:44.331 --> 00:03:47.688
the underlying asset, and simultaneously holds a

00:03:47.688 --> 00:03:51.346
perpetual future, short, against that same asset.

00:03:51.346 --> 00:03:53.549
Those two positions largely cancel

00:03:53.549 --> 00:03:55.611
each other's price risk out.

00:03:55.611 --> 00:03:59.499
The yield itself comes specifically from funding payments, paid

00:03:59.499 --> 00:04:02.841
between longs and shorts on the perpetual, a mechanism

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:05.271
covered back in Lesson three point five.

00:04:06.571 --> 00:04:09.209
Worth being precise about why this specific

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:11.608
yield source can genuinely fall all the

00:04:11.608 --> 00:04:14.066
way to zero, unlike the other three.

00:04:14.066 --> 00:04:17.503
Funding isn't fixed; it moves with market sentiment, and

00:04:17.503 --> 00:04:20.819
can turn negative during a bear market, meaning shorts

00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:23.651
pay longs instead of the other way around.

00:04:23.651 --> 00:04:26.732
When that happens, this yield source doesn't just

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:29.444
shrink, it can disappear, or even turn into

00:04:29.444 --> 00:04:32.464
an active cost, entirely independent of what the

00:04:32.464 --> 00:04:34.559
underlying asset's own price does.

00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:38.603
Worth being precise about what the word hedged

00:04:38.603 --> 00:04:41.232
actually means here, since it's easy to hear

00:04:41.232 --> 00:04:43.868
it and assume the position is risk-free.

00:04:43.868 --> 00:04:46.584
Hedged specifically means the price risk of

00:04:46.584 --> 00:04:49.723
the underlying asset is largely cancelled out.

00:04:49.723 --> 00:04:52.888
It does not mean the exchange holding those positions

00:04:52.888 --> 00:04:55.525
can't fail, and it does not mean the funding

00:04:55.525 --> 00:04:58.873
payments themselves are guaranteed to stay positive.

00:04:58.873 --> 00:05:01.328
Hedged removes one specific risk.

00:05:01.328 --> 00:05:02.978
It doesn't remove risk itself.

00:05:04.278 --> 00:05:05.368
Quick check.

00:05:05.368 --> 00:05:07.879
Why can a basis-backed stablecoin's yield

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:09.373
fall all the way to zero?

00:05:13.793 --> 00:05:16.703
The answer: funding can turn negative during

00:05:16.703 --> 00:05:19.290
a bear market, meaning shorts pay longs

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.683
instead, removing the yield entirely.

00:05:22.983 --> 00:05:23.988
Worked example.

00:05:25.288 --> 00:05:27.323
Here's the headline number first.

00:05:27.323 --> 00:05:31.223
Ten thousand dollars, deposited into a yield-bearing stablecoin

00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:34.391
paying four and a half percent, earns approximately

00:05:34.391 --> 00:05:36.763
four hundred fifty dollars a year.

00:05:36.763 --> 00:05:39.198
That's the easy part of the arithmetic.

00:05:39.198 --> 00:05:41.402
Everything that actually matters in this

00:05:41.402 --> 00:05:42.908
lesson comes right after it.

00:05:44.208 --> 00:05:46.920
Here are the three real questions to answer,

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.053
before ever buying in, regardless of how attractive

00:05:50.053 --> 00:05:52.643
that four hundred fifty dollars looks.

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:54.219
Where does the four and a half

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:56.304
percent actually come from, which of the

00:05:56.304 --> 00:05:58.688
four sources from earlier in this lesson.

00:05:58.688 --> 00:06:01.035
What happens to that rate specifically in a

00:06:01.035 --> 00:06:04.003
bear market, if it's resting on synthetic funding.

00:06:04.003 --> 00:06:06.010
And can you genuinely redeem for one

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.343
dollar, and specifically who is allowed to.

00:06:09.643 --> 00:06:13.510
Here's why that third question, about redemption, deserves just

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:16.348
as much weight as the yield source itself.

00:06:16.348 --> 00:06:19.734
Some yield-bearing stablecoins let any holder redeem

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:22.143
directly for a dollar, on demand.

00:06:22.143 --> 00:06:27.199
Others restrict redemption to specific, whitelisted institutional accounts

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:31.446
only, leaving ordinary holders dependent entirely on secondary

00:06:31.446 --> 00:06:33.603
market liquidity to exit at all.

00:06:34.903 --> 00:06:38.079
Here's what's actually at stake in dollar terms, if

00:06:38.079 --> 00:06:41.988
this specific position turned out to be synthetic, basis-backed

00:06:41.988 --> 00:06:45.769
yield, and funding genuinely went negative for a stretch.

00:06:45.769 --> 00:06:48.955
The four hundred fifty dollars a year doesn't just

00:06:48.955 --> 00:06:52.702
shrink; in a genuinely negative funding environment, it can

00:06:52.702 --> 00:06:55.888
disappear entirely, or even flip into a real cost,

00:06:55.888 --> 00:06:58.949
on the exact same ten thousand dollars, with the

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:03.071
underlying dollar peg itself potentially still holding throughout.

00:07:04.371 --> 00:07:07.504
Here's the one sentence this entire worked example

00:07:07.504 --> 00:07:10.330
exists to prove, worth holding onto more than

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.456
the specific four hundred fifty dollar figure.

00:07:13.456 --> 00:07:15.850
That number is only real for as long as

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.963
the rate itself holds, and whether it holds depends

00:07:18.963 --> 00:07:22.196
entirely on the answers to those three questions, not

00:07:22.196 --> 00:07:25.189
on how attractive four and a half percent happens

00:07:25.189 --> 00:07:26.386
to sound on its own.

00:07:27.686 --> 00:07:30.863
Worth separating even those two steadier sources from

00:07:30.863 --> 00:07:33.982
each other, since grouping them together earlier was

00:07:33.982 --> 00:07:36.453
only about how much the rate itself tends

00:07:36.453 --> 00:07:39.166
to move, not about sharing the same risk.

00:07:39.166 --> 00:07:42.847
Lending's risk lives entirely on-chain: the protocol's

00:07:42.847 --> 00:07:45.958
own code, its collateral, and its oracles.

00:07:45.958 --> 00:07:50.223
Treasury-backed risk lives largely off-chain instead: the issuer's

00:07:50.223 --> 00:07:54.233
own solvency, the custodian actually holding those treasuries,

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:57.288
and the real-world redemption terms behind them.

00:07:58.588 --> 00:08:00.048
Here's your checklist.

00:08:00.048 --> 00:08:03.193
Do it before holding any yield-bearing stablecoin.

00:08:03.193 --> 00:08:07.019
Identify its actual yield source: lending, a protocol

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:10.933
savings rate, treasury backing, or synthetic basis.

00:08:10.933 --> 00:08:13.338
Know its redemption path, and specifically

00:08:13.338 --> 00:08:15.483
who's actually allowed to use it.

00:08:15.483 --> 00:08:18.813
And split your own holdings across genuinely different

00:08:18.813 --> 00:08:22.627
yield sources, not just across different-sounding tickers that

00:08:22.627 --> 00:08:24.988
all rest on the same underlying source.

00:08:26.288 --> 00:08:29.571
Here's exactly why that last point matters so much,

00:08:29.571 --> 00:08:33.358
using two yield-bearing stablecoins both backed by the same

00:08:33.358 --> 00:08:36.373
kind of basis trade, as a concrete example.

00:08:36.373 --> 00:08:38.596
They might carry different names, different

00:08:38.596 --> 00:08:40.968
branding, and different tickers entirely.

00:08:40.968 --> 00:08:44.017
But underneath, they share the exact same yield

00:08:44.017 --> 00:08:47.193
source, and the exact same failure mode: negative

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:49.734
funding, in the exact same kind of bear

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:51.703
market, at the exact same time.

00:08:53.003 --> 00:08:54.093
Quick check.

00:08:54.093 --> 00:08:58.163
Two yield-bearing stablecoins, both backed by basis trades.

00:08:58.163 --> 00:09:00.438
Are they actually diversified from each other?

00:09:04.858 --> 00:09:06.563
The answer: not really.

00:09:06.563 --> 00:09:08.846
They share the exact same yield source,

00:09:08.846 --> 00:09:10.673
and the exact same failure mode.

00:09:11.973 --> 00:09:13.218
Let's recap.

00:09:13.218 --> 00:09:15.935
Stablecoin yield comes from one of four

00:09:15.935 --> 00:09:19.399
places: lending, a protocol savings rate, treasury

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:22.348
backing, or a synthetic basis position.

00:09:22.348 --> 00:09:24.944
Each source carries its own distinct risk,

00:09:24.944 --> 00:09:27.478
even at the exact same headline rate.

00:09:27.478 --> 00:09:29.868
Ask where the rate comes from, what happens

00:09:29.868 --> 00:09:31.605
to it in a bear market, and who

00:09:31.605 --> 00:09:34.838
can actually redeem, before the number means anything.

00:09:34.838 --> 00:09:37.262
And real diversification means different

00:09:37.262 --> 00:09:39.508
sources, never just different tickers.

00:09:40.808 --> 00:09:44.528
Identify the actual yield source before buying, know the

00:09:44.528 --> 00:09:48.678
redemption path, and diversify across sources, not tickers.

00:09:48.678 --> 00:09:50.800
Next up, Lesson 4.8: tokenized

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.538
treasuries and real-world assets (RWAs).
